Let me look at the `render_attribute` function once more. The key issue is:

```c
for(i = 0; attr->substr_offsets[i] < attr->size; i++) {
    MD_TEXTTYPE type = attr->substr_types[i];
    MD_OFFSET off = attr->substr_offsets[i];
    MD_SIZE size = attr->substr_offsets[i+1] - off;
```

The loop condition is `attr->substr_offsets[i] < attr->size`. But `attr->size` is the total size of the text. The `substr_offsets` array has `substr_count+1` entries, where `substr_offsets[substr_count] = attr->size`. So the loop runs for `i = 0` to `i = substr_count - 1`, accessing `substr_offsets[i+1]` up to `substr_offsets[substr_count]` which is valid.

Hmm, but wait. Let me reconsider. What if `attr->size` is NOT equal to `substr_offsets[substr_count]`? Let me look at the non-trivial case again:

```c
while(raw_off < raw_size) {
    if(raw_text[raw_off] == _T('\0')) {
        MD_CHECK(md_build_attr_append_substr(ctx, build, MD_TEXT_NULLCHAR, off));
        memcpy(build->text + off, raw_text + raw_off, 1);
        off++;
        raw_off++;
        continue;
    }
    ...
}
build->substr_offsets[build->substr_count] = off;
```

So `substr_offsets[substr_count] = off = attr->size`. This is consistent.

Let me look at this from a different angle. Maybe the vulnerability is in `md4c.c` itself, not in the HTML renderer. Let me search for known CVEs for md4c.

